.TH SYS_WAIT 2
.SH NAME
sys_wait \- wait for a child to end (SYS_WAIT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_wait(int pid, int *status, int flags);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_wait
collects an ended child of the caller: child
.IR pid ,
or any if
.I pid
is -1. If none has ended yet, it waits, unless
.I flags
has
.BR WAIT_NOHANG :
then it returns 0 at once. The child's exit status goes to
.I *status
(if
.I status
is not 0), and the child is gone for good.
.PP
The status is the value given to
.BR sys_exit (2)
(or returned by
.BR main ),
or, for a process a signal ended,
.BR EXIT_SIGNAL ( sig );
.BR EXIT_SIGNO ( status )
gives that signal back (0 if none), and
.B EXIT_KILLED
is
.BR EXIT_SIGNAL ( SIGKILL ).
.PP
A server that must not block learns of its children's end in its own
message loop instead
.RB ( sys_child_port (2)),
then collects them with
.BR WAIT_NOHANG .
.SH RETURN VALUE
The pid of the child collected, 0 with
.B WAIT_NOHANG
if none has ended, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ECHILD
The caller has no such child.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came while it waited.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The process is ending.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I status
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR sys_exit (2),
.BR sys_child_port (2),
.BR kill (2)
